From 52c74632e1a559a943bfd386af06f38e3b8f47b9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gavin John <gavinnjohn@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 19:46:22 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 32/41] pulse: run the stream until its clock can be trusted

The device latency cannot be known before the stream runs: while corked
PulseAudio leaves the sink and transport latencies out of its timing, and
this sink reports nothing for itself either. Measuring it by running the
stream on silence was right, but the result was sampled from Play(),
which cannot work: while the measurement is outstanding every block is
held back, so the core's FIFO fills, the decoder stalls, and Play() is
never called again. Whether the measurement finished before that happened
came down to block cadence - a property of the container, which the
device latency has nothing to do with. Measured over a fifteen second
repeat, mp3 got 1.4 seconds of audio to the device and m4a none at all,
while mkv played throughout.

Drive the silence from the write callback instead, where the server asks
for it regardless of what the decoder is doing.

Measure once per stream, too. What the device costs does not change when
the material loops, and a flush leaves the read index as far along as
ever, so the figure stays good and the next start can be timed from it
directly. Measuring again after every flush meant holding the audio back
again, and covering the wait with silence written in front of it - up to
a second and nine tenths of it, which the core then read as lateness and
flushed away.

Stop requiring the latency to be non-zero as well. A sink that really
costs nothing can never satisfy that, and so never started at all: a null
sink played fifteen seconds of silence. Whether pa_stream_get_time() has
stopped clamping is the real question, and the read index answers it.

Audio reaching the device over a fifteen second repeat: mp3 1.4s -> 14.9s,
m4a 0s -> 14.2s, ogg 3.0s -> 6.5s; mkv, flac and avi played before and
still do.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
